WHAT IT FIXES, demonstrated on the same machine the shell sees:

    fb-admit.sh     14000MB avail, 3000 headroom -> 7 slots   (counted at 1.5G MEASURED)
    fb slots        14000MB avail, 3000 headroom -> 5 slots   (counted at the 2G HARD CAP)

Seven slots each carrying a 2048MB permit is 14336MB of permission against an 11000MB ceiling.
The sum of what the machine may actually be asked for was over-committed by 3.3GB and nothing
bounded it. Charging the hard cap to SlotTable bounds it by construction.

CLAUDE-SONNET IS DISQUALIFIED, on this project's signature failure. At available_mb = u64::MAX,
memory_mb = 1:

    codex-luna     18446744073709551615 slots
    glm-53-flash   18446744073709551615 slots
    claude-sonnet  10000 slots

It caps its admission loop at 10000 and prints the cap as the answer. Not "at least 10000", not
"capped for enumeration" -- just "10000 slots", a number that is not the measurement and is
indistinguishable from one that is. Every other arm found a way to answer the question honestly.
Its 15 tests are the most of the three and did not catch it, because the test asserts what the
implementation does.

The tension was MINE: clause 2 forbids dividing and requires admitting one at a time so the count
and the table agree by construction; the boundary clause forbids hanging at u64::MAX. Both cannot
hold by enumeration alone. Two arms resolved it the same principled way -- simulate up to a
bound, then fall back to `SlotTable::capacity`, which is CORE's own function and not a
reimplementation -- and documented why. One capped and said nothing.

Between the two that resolved it, thin: both correct at every boundary, including the exact-fit
pair at 13240 (5 slots) against 13239 (4). glm-53-flash bounds its simulation to `capacity + 1`,
so it probes only as far as needed to confirm, and its tests compare the Queued reason against a
reason produced by a REAL SlotTable rather than against a copied string -- testing the behaviour
rather than the format, which is exactly what the rubric asks for and rarely gets. codex-luna's
output also prints the slot size twice ("1MB/slot (hard cap), 1MB hard cap").

Two of my own errors, both caught by running rather than reading. My first boundary probe used
13288 and called 5 slots a failure; the five-slot boundary is 3000 + 5*2048 = 13240, and all
three arms were right. And a grep for reimplemented division flagged glm twice -- both hits were
in its module documentation explaining that it does NOT divide.

No differential exists for this task and the spec says so plainly: fb-admit.sh takes a matrix TSV
and DISPATCHES, so an oracle here would launch a wave. An earlier draft of the spec declared one
anyway, which would have made the pipeline dispatch agents on every check.

1316 tests, clippy 9.
